details.image_prompt:
{
  "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two rustic, matte ceramic plates arranged with a slight depth perspective: one plate in the foreground is sharp, and the second is softly blurred in the background, together holding the total serving of eight stuffed mini pepper halves. The camera is set at a 3/4 overhead angle (40°), ensuring every component of the peppers is fully visible. Soft, natural side light from a window to the left illuminates the dish, creating subtle, realistic shadows and highlighting the fresh sheen of olive oil on the lentil mixture. The surface is a weathered, warm-toned oak tabletop with visible grain. The mini sweet peppers are halved lengthwise, forming vibrant red, orange, and yellow boats, filled with a generous, textured mixture of brown lentils, finely diced crisp green cucumber, and small, dark pieces of chopped Kalamata olives. The filling is mounded naturally within each pepper boat, showing authentic, slightly uneven distribution. Props include a small, partially used wooden spoon resting beside the front plate and a few scattered, stray lentil pieces and a stray oregano leaf on the wood surface, conveying a lived-in, honest cooking environment. The scene avoids any sterile perfection; there is a tiny, natural sauce drip on the rim of one plate. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. Warm, golden-hour color temperature, cozy and inviting. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Includes subtle traces of the cooking process like a minor crumb trail. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. Indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a high-end printed cookbook."
}
